Id: mac001w
Severity: WARN
Category: kernel
Check: check_mac
Fix: Enable AppArmor (apparmor, apparmor-profiles) or SELinux (SELINUX=enforcing), as the distribution ships.
References: apparmor(7), selinux(8)
Controls: CIS 4.1; NIST AC-3(3); ISO 8.9; CE Secure configuration

No mandatory access control system is confining anything: AppArmor and
SELinux are both off, or one is on with no policy enforced. Then a
service that is compromised can do everything the account it runs as
can, and a service running as root everything at all. With a profile,
a web server that is broken into still cannot read /etc/shadow, start a
shell, or write outside its directories.

To fix it, use the one the distribution ships: AppArmor on Debian,
Ubuntu and SUSE (install apparmor and apparmor-profiles, check with
'aa-status'), SELinux on Fedora, RHEL and their rebuilds (SELINUX=enforcing
in /etc/selinux/config, check with 'getenforce'). Both may need a kernel
parameter (apparmor=1 security=apparmor, or selinux=1) where the kernel
does not turn them on by default.
